      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(android): clarify SMS command authorization",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nCloses #91781.\n\nAndroid SMS permission and Gateway node-command authorization are separate privacy gates, but the app and generic rejection text made a granted phone permission look like a broken `sms.search` capability.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe Gateway continues to require explicit operator opt-in for private SMS contents. Dangerous-command rejection now names `gateway.nodes.allowCommands`, explicit denies name `gateway.nodes.denyCommands`, Android permission copy explains the independent Gateway gate, and node docs include a least-privilege `sms.search` example.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Read-only users can authorize `sms.search` without also enabling `sms.send`.\n- Android permission screens no longer imply that phone permission alone authorizes Gateway access.\n- Rejected invocations identify the exact policy field that needs attention.\n- Existing dangerous-command defaults and authorized invocation behavior are unchanged.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Blacksmith Testbox `tbx_01kww1as5a6jr9j1z3jeba0e43` (warmup run https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/28803749805)\n- `pnpm test src/gateway/server-methods/nodes.invoke-wake.test.ts` — 36 passed\n- `./gradlew :app:testPlayDebugUnitTest :app:testThirdPartyDebugUnitTest :app:assemblePlayDebug :app:assembleThirdPartyDebug` — passed\n- `node --import tsx scripts/native-app-i18n.ts check` — 2702 entries, unchanged\n- `pnpm docs:list` — passed\n- `oxfmt --check` on changed TypeScript and docs — passed\n- Fresh Codex autoreview after the least-privilege wording fix — clean\n\nFull Android ktlint remains blocked by unrelated existing violations in untouched main/test sources; both changed Kotlin files compiled in both flavors, and the lint report identified no violation on either changed line.\n",
